Cloudberry's singlenode tests on the port: the differences from Cloudberry's
expected output that have been reviewed, and are the port's for a reason of
its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../canon.pl gives it -- the
lines removed and added, hunk by hunk, with no file names, line numbers or
plan lines -- named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a
pass in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test
as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds
no difference or exactly the file's.

Most of every difference is a statement that fails, for a reason its error
names, and what comes of it: the rows a query over a table that was never
made would have returned, a later statement in the same transaction refused.
The reasons, by what brings them, and then each test:

  Append-optimized tables, by row and by column, are gp_ao's since M5, with
  their options, ENCODING, the bitmap index and the catalogs Cloudberry's
  tests read.  Where they still differ from Cloudberry's it is by the port's
  design of them (cloudberry.md, "gp_ao"):
    - a table's rows are in 8K pages of its own relation, after a page of
      its own, so an empty table is 8192 bytes where Cloudberry's is 0, and
      a table's size is its pages';
    - a row's TID is its segment file and its row number there, (33554432,2)
      in segment file 1 where Cloudberry's is (0,2);
    - a table by row keeps its rows as MinimalTuples, whose headers compress
      away, so get_ao_compression_ratio() differs from Cloudberry's for the
      same rows, and so does a table by column's;
    - what Cloudberry keeps in relations of each table's -- pg_aoseg's,
      pg_aovisimap_<oid> and pg_aoblkdir_<oid> and their indexes -- is in
      gp_ao's own tables, so pg_appendonly's segrelid and the rest are 0 and
      a query of such a relation finds none;
    - DELETE ... RETURNING returns the rows it deletes, where Cloudberry
      refuses it ("feature not supported on appendoptimized relations"): the
      port fetches a row through its block directory.
  And PostgreSQL 19's psql, whose \d+ of such a table has no "Compression
  Type", "Compression Level" and "Block Size" columns, and no footer, which
  Cloudberry's psql adds: the options are in pg_attribute_encoding, where the
  tests read them too.

  External tables, gpfdist, COPY ... LOG ERRORS and the error log functions
  are gp_exttable's since M5, loaded here: one node reads an external table
  itself, as Cloudberry's single-node mode does.  COPY's own FILL MISSING
  FIELDS, NEWLINE and ESCAPE outside CSV mode, which the port's external
  tables take, its COPY does not yet.

  Cloudberry's catalogs are the port's since M2, by their names, in
  pg_catalog: gp_id, gp_segment_configuration, which lists the one node,
  gp_configuration_history, written only with allow_system_table_mods, and
  gp_distribution_policy, which on one node has no row, as Cloudberry's
  single node keeps no policy (gp_catalog.c).  test_print_prefetch_joinqual
  is accepted and prints nothing: PostgreSQL's joins never prefetch their
  quals.

  Cloudberry's settings of the dispatcher and the planner are the port's
  since M2 (gp_settings.c), respelled gp.* as every one is, and a test that
  sets one runs on; on one node test_print_direct_dispatch_info prints
  nothing, as Cloudberry's single-node mode does not.

  Cloudberry's table functions, TABLE (...) and anytable: grammar the port's
  O26 does not have, and a pseudo-type an extension cannot add.

  Cloudberry's classic partition syntax, which O26 takes since M2's first step,
  where the port and Cloudberry still part:
    - ADD, DROP, ALTER and RENAME PARTITION can be PostgreSQL's too, about a
      column called "partition", which PostgreSQL 19 lets be a name and
      Cloudberry reserves.  Where PostgreSQL's grammar takes the statement
      the port leaves it PostgreSQL's, so ALTER TABLE t DROP partition is
      "column "partition" of relation "t" does not exist" where Cloudberry's
      is a syntax error, and ADD partition cc a column of type cc where
      Cloudberry's is a partition with no boundary.  Both fail in Cloudberry.
    - A SUBPARTITION TEMPLATE is kept, as written, in its table's gp label,
      and Cloudberry's catalog gp_partition_template is a view of it, which
      gives each template's relid and level as Cloudberry's does, and the
      template as pg_get_expr() prints Cloudberry's -- where a test selects
      the template itself, Cloudberry prints the dump of a node of its own.
    - An error about a partition an ALTER TABLE made is reported by
      Cloudberry at the 35th character of the statement, the length of the
      text it runs the partition's CREATE as, "(internally generated SQL
      command)"; the port reports it at the value it is about.
  Cloudberry's REVOKE says "no privileges could be revoked" when it revokes
  nothing, which PostgreSQL's does not, on any object.

  What Cloudberry's tests make with PL/Python -- count_operator(),
  find_operator(), qx_count_operator() -- counts the nodes of a plan, and so
  makes a plan an answer.  The expected output is Cloudberry's single-node
  planner's: under the port's planner a count differs where PostgreSQL 19
  plans otherwise, and under ORCA where ORCA's plan has nodes of its own --
  Dynamic Seq Scan, Dynamic Index Scan, Partition Selector -- or joins or
  aggregates otherwise.  Such a test keeps a difference for each pass.
  qx_count_operator() chooses which node to count by "Pivotal Optimizer
  (GPORCA)" in the plan, which neither Cloudberry's EXPLAIN nor the port's
  prints now ("Optimizer: GPORCA"), so under ORCA it counts the planner's
  node in ORCA's plan.

  PostgreSQL 19's own behaviour where Cloudberry changed its parser or
  PostgreSQL has changed since: a frame offset that reads a column
  ("argument of ROWS must not contain variables"), a chain of UNIONs over
  NULLs, which PostgreSQL types one UNION at a time ("UNION types text and
  integer cannot be matched"), IS NOT DISTINCT FROM printed as NOT ... IS
  DISTINCT FROM, standard_conforming_strings = off and
  escape_string_warning, which PostgreSQL 19 no longer has, NOT NULL
  constraints, which PostgreSQL 18 keeps in pg_constraint, and "operator
  does not exist", which PostgreSQL 19 explains in a DETAIL and a HINT where
  PostgreSQL 14 had one HINT.  And of partitioned tables: none may be
  UNLOGGED ("partitioned tables cannot be unlogged"), an exclusion
  constraint on one is allowed (PostgreSQL 17), "UNIQUE constraint" or
  "PRIMARY KEY constraint on partitioned table must include all partitioning
  columns" names the constraint's kind, and "cannot alter column ... because
  it is part of the partition key" has a caret; PUBLIC may no longer CREATE
  in schema public (PostgreSQL 15), so a table a role of the test's makes
  there is refused; and psql's \d+ lists each partition on a line of its
  own, under "Partitions:", its \dt is headed "List of tables" and has no
  Storage column, and its \dm is headed "List of materialized views".  A
  NOTICE that CREATE FUNCTION has made a shell type names the line and
  column of the type it made.  And cursors: one PostgreSQL can scroll -- one
  whose plan sorts -- is scrollable, where Cloudberry makes none so, and
  FETCH BACKWARD in one that is not says "cursor can only scan forward",
  where Cloudberry's says "backward scan is not supported in this version of
  Apache Cloudberry".

  DECODE and CASE x WHEN IS NOT DISTINCT FROM y, which Cloudberry's parser
  makes a CASE with an operand and the port's rewrite a searched CASE with
  the operand in each arm, because PostgreSQL 19 compares a CASE's operand
  with = and nothing else: a view prints the searched CASE, and the operand
  is evaluated in each arm the CASE tries rather than once.  The answers are
  Cloudberry's, and so are the errors and where they are reported.

The tests

AOCO_Compression  psql's \d+ of its tables by column (above), which is most
  of it; a NOTICE's position; and the sizes and compression ratios of
  append-optimized tables.
AORO_Compression  The size and compression ratio of an append-optimized
  table.
aocs  aototal(), which reads pg_aoseg's relation of a table, and the indexes
  of pg_aovisimap_<oid> and pg_aoblkdir_<oid>; a TID; a compression ratio;
  psql's \d+; and a cursor's scrolling.
bfv_aggregate (planner, orca)  A frame offset that reads a column; and
  under ORCA the GroupAggregate and HashAggregate count_operator() counts,
  which ORCA chooses otherwise.
bfv_catalog  VACUUM FULL of pg_stat_last_operation, Cloudberry's catalog of
  when an object was last changed, which is gp_core's view of a table of
  its own here, and skipped, so it leaves no VACUUM row of itself;
  escape_string_warning and standard_conforming_strings = off, and one row
  more in a query over pg_constraint: the table's NOT NULL constraint.
bfv_partition_plans (orca)  The Dynamic Seq Scans, Dynamic Index Scans and
  Partition Selectors that count_operator() and find_operator() find under
  ORCA, where Cloudberry's planner makes none.
case_gp  The deparse of IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, and of the CASE form, which
  prints as the searched CASE it was rewritten into; blip(1), the operand of
  a CASE with three arms, called three times where Cloudberry calls it once,
  so calls_to_blip has seven rows where it has five and the CASE's answer is
  the same 666; and PostgreSQL 19's DETAIL for "operator does not exist",
  twice.
createdb  gp_inject_fault('all', 'reset') over gp_segment_configuration,
  which lists the port's one node where Cloudberry's single node has a
  standby coordinator too (gpdemo's WITH_STANDBY): one "Success:" where the
  expected output has two.  gp.select_invisible, which the test sets to
  find the failed CREATE DATABASE's row and look for its directories, shows
  an append-optimized table's deleted rows here (gp_ao) and no heap table's
  aborted ones, so the row is not found, where Cloudberry finds it and no
  directory; greenplum's createdb looks for them on every node of a cluster.
gp_gin_index  the complex type, which Cloudberry's own earlier tests make;
  and the index wowidx and the rows of test_tsvector that PostgreSQL 19's
  tsearch test, which runs before it here, leaves behind (two counts one and
  three higher).
gp_recursive_cte  a plan gpdiff.pl does not take for one, which is
  PostgreSQL 19's planner's.
gpcopy  A backslash before a line's end, which Cloudberry's COPY ends the
  line at and PostgreSQL's reads as an escaped newline; a newline of another
  kind than NEWLINE names, which Cloudberry's COPY reads as data and
  PostgreSQL's refuses ("literal newline found in data"); the broken_int4
  type of Cloudberry's regress.so; and GP_DATABASE, one of the variables
  Cloudberry gives a COPY's PROGRAM, which the port's does not.
incremental_analyze  Its database is made from template1, which has
  gp_core's extension and the others (../sql/gp_setup.sql), so its ANALYZE
  is Cloudberry's there, a root's statistics its leaves' merged.  What
  differs: a column of 400 kB values -- its width 1587, the size PostgreSQL
  19 stores such a value compressed in, where Cloudberry's is 4591; and the
  root's number of distinct values of it 0, where Cloudberry's single node
  says 1: Cloudberry adds every value to a leaf's HyperLogLog counter, as
  the bytes it is stored in, compressed, before it passes over a value wider
  than 1 kB (compute_scalar_stats(), analyze.c), and the port leaves such a
  value out of the counter by its size (gp_partmerge.c), as Cloudberry's
  segments leave it out on a cluster.  VERBOSE's pages scanned, 8 kB pages
  where Cloudberry's are 32 kB; and an append-optimized partition's pages,
  gp_ao's 8 kB pages of its file, as in the greenplum suite's
  incremental_analyze.
matview_ao  psql's \dm.
partition  Most reasons above: its templates, which gp_partition_template shows;
  Cloudberry's REVOKE notice; an exclusion constraint PostgreSQL 19 allows;
  "PRIMARY KEY constraint on partitioned table"; the caret of an error in a
  partition an ALTER TABLE made; psql's \d+ and \dt.
partition1  PostgreSQL's reading of ALTER TABLE ... ADD partition and DROP
  partition; the wording and caret of PostgreSQL 19's partition key errors;
  psql's \d+.
partition_ddl  "operator does not exist" explained in a DETAIL, and
  PostgreSQL's reading of ALTER TABLE ... ADD partition.
partition_indexing  "UNIQUE constraint on partitioned table".
partition_storage  psql's \d+ of append-optimized tables.
partition_unlogged  an unlogged partitioned table, which PostgreSQL 19 has
  none of.
qp_query_execution (orca)  qx_count_operator() of its hash joins and nested
  loops, which ORCA plans otherwise, counted by the planner's name for each
  (see above).
resource_queue  SHOW resource_scheduler, whose header is the port's name
  of it, gp.resource_scheduler.
returning_gp  DELETE ... RETURNING of an append-optimized table.
singlenode_compatibility_cbdb  the warning Cloudberry's single-node mode
  gives for DISTRIBUTED BY (the port records the policy, for ORCA and M2),
  and TABLE (...) and anytable.  ALTER TABLE ... SET DISTRIBUTED BY is
  refused as Cloudberry refuses it in single-node mode.  Under ORCA the same
  differences, which gpdiff.pl places elsewhere -- it pairs the count of
  the append-optimized table aocs1 with Cloudberry's there -- so that pass
  has a file of its own (singlenode_compatibility_cbdb.orca.diff).
tag  Its catalogs are views here, of the port's labels (gp_sql's script
  makes pg_tag, pg_tag_description and Cloudberry's five *_tag_descriptions
  in pg_catalog), so \d+ shows a view where Cloudberry's shows a table.
  other_db, the database the test makes, has them from template1
  (../sql/gp_setup.sql), and finds the tags another database made there:
  the definitions are the cluster's, as Cloudberry's are, whichever
  database makes them.
  CREATE TAG, ALTER TAG and DROP TAG are CALLs of gp_sql's procedures, and
  an error from one is Cloudberry's message, with a CONTEXT naming gp_sql's
  functions.  A malformed TAG clause (TAG; or TAG ()) is PostgreSQL's error
  at the word the rewrite left -- a syntax error, or "unrecognized role
  option" of a role's -- where Cloudberry's syntax error is at the next.
union_gp (planner, orca)  a chain of UNIONs over NULLs; and under the
  planner count_operator() of an Append, one where Cloudberry's planner has
  two: PostgreSQL 19 pulls a WITH query's UNION ALL up into the UNION ALL
  that reads it, and ORCA, like Cloudberry, does not.
